            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Overall, your house has a moderate amount of air leakage, however there are some key areas where air leakage occurs. 



Air leakage was observed through electrical outlets on your exterior walls, the weather stripping along the exterior doors, and at the top of the walls where the walls meet the ceilings. I recommend that you outfit electrical outlets located along the exterior walls with an electrical wall plate gasket. 



I recommend keeping the weather stripping along all of your exterior doors in good shape Using a screw in aluminum style weather stripping may help to create a better seal, as well as using an adjustable strike plate to ensure the door makes good contact with the weather stripping while closed. 



Air leakage was observed along the baseboards in the dining room, which may be part of the reason this area feels cold. 

Air leakage was also observed through both of the bathroom exhaust fans. 



I recommend when you are purchasing your exhaust fans that you choose ENERGY STAR?????? rated models, which will have a new damper built into the new unit.



Be aware that other upgrades suggested in this report may also affect your air leakage rate.



Savings: For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially save $70 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 2.2 GJ, and qualify for up to$500 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program.



To be eligible for an air sealing rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, exterior main wall insulation is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x6 studs with 6 inches (R20) of fiberglass batt insulation, and 2x2 interior strapping with R8 insulation. I have modelled adding 3 inches (R15) of extruded polystyrene insulation to all exterior walls of the home. I recommend that when you do this upgrade, you extend the new insulation and siding down over the header space between your crawlspace and the main floor of your house.



Savings:

By adding R15 of extruded polystyrene insulation, you could potentially save $270 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 8.1 GJ, and qualify for up to$2500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program

Under the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, the rebate you receive for insulation will be double that of the AEA?????????s rebate program for energy-efficient products (the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program).



To be eligible for an insulation rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



</mainWalls>
                <foundation>



While I was in your crawlspace, I noticed that the header space in your front porch appears to be uninsulated. I recommend that you add R20 insulation to this space from the inside. As discussed in your wall upgrade section, I also recommend that you extend the wall insulation over the header when you are adding exterior wall insulation. 



I also noticed a musty smell while I was down in your crawlspace. I recommend that you add venting to the crawlspace walls that can be opened in the summer to allow the crawlspace to dry out, and then be closed in the winter to prevent heat loss. 



You do already have two insulated ducts going down into the crawl space to provide ventilation, but they are very close together causing a short circuit situation. I recommend that you block off one of these ducts and install a similar duct on the other side of your house, with the interior ends of the ducts far enough apart to create a cross breeze. </foundation>
                <windows>

As all of your windows are older double pane ones that do not appear to have a Low E coating or an insulating gas fill between the panes of glass, I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading all your windows with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows. 



Window retrofits are best done at the same time as an exterior siding retrofit; however you may wish to replace the two broken windows earlier. 



Savings: 

By replacing all your windows with triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged, ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially save $350 per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by 10.6 GJ.  Windows that are ENERGY STAR?????? qualified and rated for climate zone 3 are eligible for $250 per window in rebates, to a max of 12 windows through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program. 



To be eligible for a windows rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, exterior main wall insulation is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</windows>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your current oil fired furnace was originally rated at 82.1% AFUE. When it is time to replace it, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency oil furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, which also incorporates an ECM fan blower motor. If you choose a high-efficiency oil furnace (direct vented out a side wall), the current chimney can be eliminated altogether.



Savings:

By replacing your heating system with a sealed combustion oil-fired furnace with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, that also incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $680 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 18.9 GJ, and qualify for up to $1,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program

Under the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, the rebate you receive for the furnace will be double that of the AEA?????????s rebate program for energy-efficient products (the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program).



To be eligible for a furnace rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, exterior main wall insulation is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

ADDITIONAL COMMENTS



WOOD STOVE

As you are planning on upgrading your wood stove, I have modelled the potential savings. AEA recommends a ULC Certified wood stove. Ensure that the installation complies with local building and fire codes, and includes a dedicated combustion air duct.



As your house is under renovations, I strongly recommend that you install new smoke detectors and a new carbon monoxide alarm before moving back in. 



Savings:

By installing a Certified wood stove and using it for 25% of your heating load, you could potentially save $700 per year in energy savings, and qualify for up to 1/2 of pre-tax purchase cost of the stove to a maximum of $1,000 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program





FUEL COSTS

The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Fort Simpson:

Electricity - $18.90 min, $0.31 per kWh

Oil  - $1.27 per litre 

Propane - $0.865 per litre

Wood - $315.00 per cord 

Pellets - $7.35 per bag

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



DISCLAIMER

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.

